\details{
Since SAGEE only specifies the marginal mean, the marginal variance, and a working correlation, there is no true likelihood function. Instead, the function returns the so-called "pseudo log-likelihood", defined as
\deqn{
Q(\Psi) = -\frac{1}{2} \sum_{i} \left\{ r_i^\top R_{g_i}^{-1} r_i + \sum_{s} \log v_{si} + \log |R_{g_i}| \right\},
}
where \eqn{r_i} denotes the vector of Pearson residuals for species \eqn{i}, \eqn{v_{si}} is its fitted marginal variance at site \eqn{s}, and \eqn{R_{g_i}} is the working correlation matrix of the corresponding assigned archetype. The constant involving \eqn{2\pi} is omitted. Note the above is the same criterion used to assign species to archetypes as part of the SAGEE model fitting process.

The \code{"df"} attribute counts the number of estimated parameters as follows:
\itemize{
\item The number of archetypal and species-specific coefficients, i.e., \code{length(object$archetypal_coefficients) + length(object$species_coefficients)}.
\item Any estimated species-specific dispersion and power parameters.
\item The number of working correlation parameters, i.e., the spatial range and spatial proportion when \code{cor_type = "matern"} or \code{cor_type = "NNGP"}, either once if \code{common = TRUE} or once per archetype if \code{common = FALSE}. No parameters are counted for \code{cor_type = "independence"} or \code{cor_type = "supplied"}.
}
}
\note{
Because the result is an object of class \code{"logLik"} with a \code{"df"} attribute, then functions such as \code{\link[stats:AIC]{stats::AIC()}} can be applied to a fitted SAGEE model. However, these \strong{should be taken with a large grain of salt} as standard information criteria are not formally justified for a pseudo log-likelihood.
